Idempotency Middleware — Detailed Explanation
This is a clean, functional implementation of the idempotency guard. Let me dissect it line by line, explain the flow, and — importantly — point out some production concerns that this simplified version hints at.

What This Middleware Does (In One Sentence)
"If a request with this Idempotency-Key has been seen before, replay the saved response instead of processing the payment again."

This prevents a double charge when a client retries after a network timeout or crash.

 Full Walkthrough
The Function Signature
go
func Idempotency(db *sql.DB, next http.Handler) http.Handler
Takes the database connection (db *sql.DB) and the next handler in the chain
Returns a new http.Handler that wraps the original one
This is the middleware pattern — it intercepts the request before passing it down the chain
Step 1 — Extract and Validate the Key
go
key := r.Header.Get("Idempotency-Key")
if key == "" {
    http.Error(w, "Missing Idempotency-Key", http.StatusBadRequest)
    return
}
What happens: Read the Idempotency-Key header.

If it's absent → reject with 400 Bad Request immediately. No payment logic runs.
If present → proceed.
Why this is required: Every payment request must carry an idempotency key. Rejecting requests without one is a deliberate design decision — it forces all clients to participate in the idempotency contract.

Step 2 — Query the Database for an Existing Key
go
var response []byte
err := db.QueryRow("SELECT response FROM idempotency_keys WHERE key=$1", key).Scan(&response)
What happens: Look up the key in the idempotency_keys table. If a row exists, retrieve the previously-stored response body.

Step 3 — Replay Path (Key already exists)
go
if err == nil {
    w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
    w.Write(response)
    return
}
This is the "no double charge" branch.

Situation	Behavior
err == nil	Row found → replay the stored response
err == sql.ErrNoRows	Key is new → fall through and process
err is something else	(e.g., DB outage) → problem, see concerns below
The stored response is written back verbatim. The client gets the exact same result as the original request — so from the client's perspective, a retry is indistinguishable from the first attempt.

Step 4 — Processing Path (New key — first occurrence)
go
rw := &responseWriter{ResponseWriter: w}
next.ServeHTTP(rw, r)
Here's the clever part. It wraps the real ResponseWriter in a custom responseWriter so the downstream handler's output can be captured before it's stored.

next.ServeHTTP(rw, r) — passes the request (and the wrapped writer) down to the actual payment handler.
The handler writes its response to rw, which snapshots the body (see the responseWriter type below).
Step 5 — Store the Response Keyed by Idempotency Key
go
respBytes, _ := json.Marshal(rw.body)
_, _ = db.Exec("INSERT INTO idempotency_keys(key, response) VALUES($1, $2)", key, respBytes)
Convert the captured response body to JSON bytes.
INSERT a new row: key → response.
Next time this key appears, Step 3 will find it and replay.
The Helper Type: responseWriter
go
type responseWriter struct {
    http.ResponseWriter   // embeds the original writer (satisfies the interface)
    body interface{}      // captures the JSON response
}

func (rw *responseWriter) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {
    json.Unmarshal(b, &rw.body)   // capture JSON into body
    return rw.ResponseWriter.Write(b)  // ALSO forward to the real client
}
The magic: By embedding http.ResponseWriter, this struct automatically satisfies the interface — so it can be passed to any handler expecting
